          <SocialProof users="13.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-filters-matter" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Filters Decide Everything</a>
          <a href="#filter-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Four Filter Types</a>
          <a href="#bags" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Collection Bags</a>
          <a href="#when-to-clean" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Cleaning and Replacing</a>
          <a href="#matching-filter-to-job" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Matching Filter to Job</a>
          <a href="#costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What They Cost</a>
          <a href="#storage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Storage and Habits</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Nearly every shop vac problem people describe — lost suction, dust blown back into the room, a smell that will not go away, a motor that died young — is a filter problem. It is the cheapest component on the machine and the one that decides how the expensive parts behave.
        </p>

        <div id="why-filters-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why the Filter Decides How Your Shop Vac Performs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shop vac is a simple machine with one component that determines almost everything about how it behaves: the filter. It sets what the machine captures, what it blows back into the room, how long suction holds up during a job, and whether the motor survives the work you are doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mechanism is worth understanding because it explains nearly every complaint people have. Air is pulled through the filter, and the dust it stops accumulates on the filter surface. As that layer builds, the air path through the pleats narrows, and airflow falls. This is called blinding, and it happens far faster on fine dust than on chips — a cartridge that handles a year of leaves can lose half its airflow in twenty minutes of drywall sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second reason it matters is health. The visible debris in the tank is not the dangerous fraction. The particles small enough to stay airborne and reach deep into the lungs are the ones a filter either captures or exhausts back into the room at head height, and a standard shop vac filter is not designed to capture them.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule That Solves Most Filter Problems</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Use a collection bag with the cartridge, not instead of it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The bag catches the bulk of the debris before it ever reaches the pleats, which keeps airflow high through a whole job and extends cartridge life by many times. Nearly every "my shop vac lost suction" problem traces back to running bagless on fine dust.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="filter-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Filter Types and What Each One Is For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most shop vacs accept several filter options from the same manufacturer, and they are not interchangeable in purpose.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stops</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use It For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wet Use?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard pleated cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chips, leaves, coarse dry debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General garage and yard work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — remove first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine dust cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most household dust, sanding dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indoor cleanup, woodworking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HEPA-rated cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silica, fine wood dust, allergens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Masonry, renovation, allergy homes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foam sleeve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing fine; protects the motor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet pickup only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes — this is the wet filter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The foam sleeve is the one people misunderstand. It is not a filter in any meaningful sense; it is a barrier that keeps splashing water and wet debris out of the motor housing while the machine picks up liquid. It captures almost nothing fine, which is fine, because water pickup does not create airborne dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pleated cartridge and the foam sleeve are alternatives, not partners. Running a paper or pleated cartridge during wet pickup soaks it, collapses the media and ruins it in one use — and a sodden filter also chokes airflow so completely that the machine will barely pick up at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            HEPA-rated cartridges are the right choice for anything mineral. Concrete, tile, mortar and brick dust all contain crystalline silica, which is a recognised respiratory hazard rather than a nuisance, and a standard filter passes a meaningful fraction of it straight through.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="bags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Collection Bags: The Accessory That Changes Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            A collection bag sits inside the tank and receives the debris before it reaches the cartridge. It is the single highest-value accessory for a shop vac and the one most owners skip because the machine works without it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three things improve immediately when you fit one. Suction holds up through a job rather than tailing off, because the pleats stay clean. Filter life extends from weeks to many months, which more than pays for the bags. And emptying becomes a matter of lifting a sealed bag out rather than tipping a drum and releasing a cloud of exactly the dust you spent an hour collecting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Bag Is Essential</h3>
          <p>
            Drywall sanding, plaster, concrete dust, ash, and any indoor cleanup where you do not want a haze afterwards. In each of these the dust is fine enough to blind a cartridge quickly and fine enough to matter to your lungs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Can Skip It</h3>
          <p>
            Wet pickup — bags and water do not mix, and the paper simply disintegrates. Also bulky dry debris where the material is coarse and the tank will be emptied anyway: leaves, wood offcuts, gravel, packing material. Here a bag fills quickly with material that would never have troubled the filter.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical note: bags are model-specific and the fit matters. A bag that does not seal properly around the inlet lets debris bypass it entirely, which delivers the cost with none of the benefit. Buy the bags listed for your model rather than a universal pack that nearly fits.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="when-to-clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cleaning, Tapping and Knowing When to Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pleated cartridges are cleanable up to a point, and how you clean them determines whether you get one more job or one more year out of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gentlest and most effective method is tapping. Take the cartridge outside, hold it by the plastic end cap, and tap it firmly against a hard surface to release the dust cake from the pleats. Do this between sessions rather than when suction has already gone, and most of the airflow comes back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compressed air works and needs care: blow from the inside outward, at moderate pressure, holding the nozzle several inches away. Blowing hard from the outside drives the dust deeper into the media and can tear the pleats, which finishes the filter permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Washing is only appropriate for cartridges explicitly marked washable. Where it is allowed, rinse from the inside out with low-pressure water, then let it dry completely — genuinely completely, over a day or more — before refitting. A damp cartridge fitted back into a vac develops mildew, blinds immediately, and smells for the rest of its life.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">When to Replace Rather Than Clean</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Once cleaning no longer restores airflow, or the pleats are torn, soft or permanently grey.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Filters are consumables. A cartridge that has been cleaned a dozen times has media damage you cannot see, and continuing to use it means both weak suction and dust reaching the motor. Replacement cartridges cost a fraction of the machine.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="matching-filter-to-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Matching the Filter to the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the whole skill, and it takes ten seconds each time.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Bag?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water and spills</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foam sleeve only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leaves, chips, gravel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optional</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Workshop sawdust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dust cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall and plaster</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dust or HEPA cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, essential</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, tile, masonry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HEPA cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, essential</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold fireplace ash</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HEPA cartridge, or an ash vac</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Car interiors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dust cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optional</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Ash deserves a specific warning. Fireplace and wood stove ash is extremely fine, it packs a filter faster than almost anything else, and embers hidden in ash have started fires inside shop vac tanks. Only ever vacuum ash that has been cold for several days, use a metal-canister ash vacuum if you do it regularly, and never use a bag rated only for dust with material you are not certain is cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switching between wet and dry work is the other routine changeover. Dry the tank before refitting a pleated cartridge, because residual water wicks straight into the pleats and destroys them. This is the most common way a good cartridge dies.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Filters and Bags Actually Cost to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consumables on a shop vac are modest, and running the right ones costs less than running the wrong ones badly.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lasts</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard pleated cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12-$25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years with a bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine dust cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20-$35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 years with a bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">HEPA cartridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$35-$70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 years with a bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foam sleeve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8-$15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collection bags</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3-$6 each</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One job to several months</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The arithmetic favours bags heavily. A $5 bag that triples the life of a $30 fine dust cartridge pays for itself several times over, and that ignores the time saved not cleaning a blinded filter mid-job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party filters are a mixed picture. For standard cartridges they are generally fine and considerably cheaper. For HEPA-rated cartridges, buy the manufacturer's part or a brand that publishes an actual filtration standard — an unrated 'HEPA-style' cartridge is a marketing phrase rather than a specification, and the whole point of buying one is the rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep one spare cartridge on the shelf. The moment you need a clean filter is always mid-job, and a vac with a blinded filter and no replacement is a vac that stops being used.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Storage and Habits That Extend Filter Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            Store the machine with the filter dry and the tank empty. Debris left in the tank holds moisture against the cartridge base, and a damp cartridge is a dead cartridge within a few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the foam sleeve and the pleated cartridge separately rather than stacked, and let the foam dry fully after wet work — it is the component that most often grows mildew, and the smell transfers into everything the machine exhausts afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the vac for thirty seconds with a clean filter after a wet job to dry the impeller housing and the airflow path. It costs nothing and prevents the musty smell that otherwise announces itself the next time you switch on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, get into the habit of checking the filter before a job rather than during one. Ninety seconds with the cartridge out, tapped clean and refitted, is the difference between a machine that pulls properly for an hour and one that quietly gives up ten minutes in and leaves you wondering whether it is time to buy a new vac. In our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac comparison</a>, the machines that impressed most were mostly the ones that made this check easy — a filter you can reach in seconds gets checked, and one buried behind a clamped lid does not.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Bagless on Fine Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the root of most 'my shop vac lost suction' complaints. Without a bag, fine dust loads the pleats directly and airflow can halve within twenty minutes on drywall or plaster. A few dollars of bag protects a far more expensive cartridge and keeps the machine working through the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Paper Cartridge In for Wet Pickup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pleated cartridge soaks, collapses and is ruined in one wet job, and while it is wet the machine barely picks up at all. Remove it, fit the foam sleeve, and dry the tank before the cartridge goes back in for dry work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blowing Compressed Air Into the Filter From Outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-pressure air aimed at the outside of the pleats drives dust deeper into the media and can tear it. Clean from the inside outward at moderate pressure, several inches away — or better, just tap the cartridge against a hard surface, which works nearly as well and cannot damage it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Ash That Is Not Stone Cold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Embers concealed in fireplace ash have started fires inside shop vac tanks, and ash packs a filter faster than almost anything else. Wait several days, use a HEPA cartridge and a bag, and use a metal-canister ash vacuum if this is a regular job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refitting a Damp Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cartridge that feels almost dry is not dry. Refitted damp, it blinds immediately, grows mildew, and permanently smells. Dry filters fully over a day or more, and dry the tank after wet work before the pleated cartridge goes anywhere near it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a filter in my shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For dry pickup, always — without one, debris goes straight through the impeller and into the motor housing, and fine dust is exhausted back into the room. The only exception is wet pickup, where the pleated cartridge must be removed and, on models that use one, a foam sleeve fitted in its place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a shop vac filter and a bag?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The cartridge filters the air leaving the machine; the bag collects the debris before it reaches the cartridge. They work together rather than as alternatives. Adding a bag keeps the pleats clean, holds suction up through a whole job, extends filter life several times over, and makes emptying a dust-free operation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you wash a shop vac filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is marked washable. Where it is, rinse from the inside outward at low pressure and let it dry completely — over a day or more — before refitting, because a damp cartridge blinds instantly and grows mildew. For non-washable cartridges, tap the dust cake out against a hard surface instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What filter do I use for drywall dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fine dust or HEPA-rated cartridge, always with a collection bag. Drywall dust is fine enough to pass through a standard filter, blind it within minutes, and hang in the air afterwards. Without a bag, expect suction to fall off quickly and a haze of dust to settle over everything in the room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which filter do I use for vacuuming water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Remove the pleated or paper cartridge entirely and fit the foam sleeve if your model uses one. A pleated filter soaks, collapses and is ruined in a single wet pickup, and a sodden filter chokes airflow so completely that the machine barely lifts water at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace a shop vac filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a bag fitted, a standard or fine dust cartridge commonly lasts one to three years of domestic use. Replace it sooner when cleaning no longer restores airflow, or when the pleats are torn, soft or permanently grey. Filters are consumables, and a tired one means both weak suction and dust reaching the motor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are HEPA shop vac filters worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anything mineral, yes. Concrete, tile, mortar and brick dust contain crystalline silica, which is a recognised respiratory hazard, and a standard cartridge passes a meaningful fraction of it back into the room. Buy a cartridge with a published filtration rating rather than one described as 'HEPA-style', which is marketing rather than a standard.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

